  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nShowers and a few weak t-storms persist near and north of I-80\nas of early afternoon. This will gradually clear through the\nafternoon, but lingering cloud cover will keep our\nnorthern/eastern zones significantly cooler than further\nsouthwest that have seen mostly sunny skies nearly all day. \n\nAfter a dry evening, scattered showers and a few t-storms\nredevelop early Thursday morning, aided by convergence in the\n850-700mb layer. Instability is limited, so severe weather is \nnot expected, but this does have the potential to be produce \nheavier rain than Wednesday morning. Based on the 12Z HREF, localized\nrain totals of 1.00-2.50\" are possible (10% chance), with more \nwidespread totals in the 0.15-0.75\" range. \n\nMuch like Wednesday, rain will clear out in the afternoon,\nallowing temperatures to rebound to the mid 70s to near 90\ndegrees (warmest in the southwest). One more round of showers\nand thunderstorms is expected to develop Thursday evening. \nGiven the relative warmth in portions of KS, a few strong storms\ncannot be ruled out in these areas, but widespread strong to \nsevere storms are not expected. \n\nFriday will dry out and trend warmer, and this trend will\ncontinue into the weekend. Widespread highs in the 90s are\nexpected to return for Saturday, and many areas will approach \n100 degrees Sunday and Monday. Relatively high surface moisture \nwill result in quite high heat index values. At this point, it \nappears likely (70%) that we will exceed 105 degree heat \nindices, and 110 degree heat indices are very possible (30%).\n\n\nThis wave of extreme heat looks to be relatively short-lived, \nthough. Global ensembles favor a return to more seasonable \nwarmth for the middle to end of next week. This will also be\naccompanied by additional chances for rain/t-storms, although\nthere is some uncertainty on how widespread/beneficial these\nwill be.",
